# Line endings: LF in the repository, LF in every working tree.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which hands the working tree
# CRLF. Any test that asserts on a `\n`-anchored line of a file it read from
# disk then fails on Windows and nowhere else. That class of failure reached
# CI repeatedly and was only visible in the weekly cross-platform sweep, up to
# a week after the commit that caused it.
#
# The index already stores LF, so this governs checkout only: no
# re-normalisation, no diff churn.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the one text format that requires CRLF to be correct.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Byte-exact, never normalised. Git's content heuristic gets most of these
# right on its own, but not all of them — a PDF whose streams are uncompressed
# carries no early NUL and is detected as text — so the intent is stated here
# rather than left to the heuristic.
*.a     -text
*.bin   -text
*.dll   -text
*.dmg   -text
*.docx  -text
*.dylib -text
*.exe   -text
*.fst   -text
*.ghw   -text
*.gif   -text
*.gz    -text
*.icns  -text
*.ico   -text
*.jar   -text
*.jpeg  -text
*.jpg   -text
*.lxt   -text
*.lxt2  -text
*.mov   -text
*.mp4   -text
*.otf   -text
*.pdf   -text
*.png   -text
*.pyc   -text
*.riv   -text
*.so    -text
*.ttf   -text
*.vvp   -text
*.wasm  -text
*.webm  -text
*.webp  -text
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text
*.zip   -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules below predate the blanket rule above and remain authoritative: within
# a .gitattributes the last matching pattern wins, so the exception at the
# foot of this file still governs the files it names.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Fixture corpora are replayed byte-for-byte by the demo-mode drivers and
# re-derived into committed goldens. Git for Windows defaults to
# `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this the windows-latest CI job would
# check the corpora out with different bytes than the ubuntu and macos
# jobs parse. Pin them to LF so a fixture is the same file on every runner.
#
# `SbyLogReader` is CRLF-tolerant regardless (it splits with `LineSplitter`
# and trims each line), and that tolerance is tested — this is belt to that
# braces, and it keeps the goldens honest.
test/fixtures/**    text eol=lf
verification/**     text eol=lf

# Exception to the rule above, and for the same reason it exists. The two
# `format_variance_*` DUTs encode line-ending variance as *test data*: their
# `case.json` states they are the golden's words "re-spelled: CRLF line
# endings, blank and whitespace-only lines, uppercase hex, 0X prefixes", and
# the `riscv_signature` profile is asserted to fold all four. `eol=lf` would
# rewrite the very bytes under test — silently, since the suite would still
# pass with one of the four variances gone. Pin them to verbatim instead,
# which delivers the same "identical on every runner" guarantee more strongly
# than `eol=lf` does: no conversion on any platform, in either direction.
#
# Left as `text eol=lf` these are also permanently dirty in every clone: git
# normalises on the way in but never strips CR on checkout, so the worktree
# never matches the CRLF blob. That is what kept the submodule detached.
verification/fixtures/golden_compare/format_variance_*/dut.sig    -text
